  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe core idea is to iteratively optimize the step function over increasingly finer resolution grids to find a lower Erd\u0151s constant. We start with the provided 42-point parent solution and gradually refine it by:\n- Tripling the grid resolution each generation (starting from 42, then 126, 378, etc.)\n- Maintaining the circular structure while allowing for localized optimizations\n- Using a circle wave construction to initialize each resolution level\n- Employing a multi-start local search with grid morphing\n\nThe key challenges addressed are:\n- Avoiding local minima through multi-resolution exploration\n- Maintaining structural integrity while improving the overlap constant\n- Ensuring mathematical correctness in the optimization\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: Partitioned continuous parent circle wave solution seeks to minimize maximum cross-overlap\n- Objective: C5 = dx * max_k (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))\n- Constraints:\n  \u2211h_i = n_points/2 (normalized integral)\n  0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- Optimization: Curvilinear search through local refinement\n- Solution representation: Step function samples h_i\n- Dimensionality: Native grid resolution (adaptive)\n- Objective evaluation: Linear correlation based on grid representation\n- Box constraints handling: Gradient-based with projection post-step\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Input: seed, budget_s\nb. Setup:\n   - Initial grid = provided parent (n=42)\n   - Generate circle wave initial guess at each resolution\nc. Evolution:\n   1. For current resolution level (starting triple increment):\n        - Initialize population with:\n            \u25b6 Circle wave structure + small random init\n            \u25b6 3-point interpolation of parent (no-wrap)\n   2. Local search:\n        \u25b6 Perform line search along expected steepest descent\n        \u25b6 Clip & normalize to satisfy integral constraint\n        \u25b6 Consider grid morphing (relatively shifted)\n   3. Mutation:\n        \u25b6 Exchange randomly morphed segments from best solutions\n        \u25b6 Maintain diversity with random restarts\n   4. Selection:\n        \u25b6 Prefer lower C5 values\n        \u25b6 Preserve structural properties\nd. Objective & Repair:\n   - Compute C5 via: dx * max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full'))\n   - Exact repair: project to satisfy \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n   - Validation: Return only valid solutions\n   - Fallback: parent solution if search unsuccessful\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\na. Primary variation: Tripling resolution each generation\nb. Initialization:\n   - Circle wave: h_i = cos(2\u03c0*i/period)\n   - Period scaling with resolution\nc. Local update:\n   - Line search: .3-Optimizer-boundable step function\n   - Morph operators: Grid shifting \u00d70.2-0.6\n   - Repair: Constraint projection maintaining [0,1]\nd. Population size: 3\u00d7 per generation (growing with resolution)\ne. Stopping rules:\n   - Time budget\n   - Iteration cap\n   - Minimal improvement threshold\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\na. Dominant cost: Objective function calls (O(n_points\u00b2))\nb. Main timings: \n   - Initial 42-points \u2248 0.5s batch\n   - Each resolution step \u2248 prior^3\u00b2 scaling\nc. Scheduling:\n   - 100s for initial fine-tune\n   - 500s for first resolution triple (42\u2192126)\n   - 480s for balancing budget across levels\nd. Resource handling:\n   - Early termination on significant progress stall\n   - Gradient violation recovery via repair\n   - Memory tracking for adaptive workload\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\na. Feasibility check:\n   \u25b6 all(0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1)\n   \u25b6 abs(\u2211h_i - n_points/2) < 1e-4\nb. Objective evaluation:\n   \u25b6 Explicit cross-calculation\nc. Format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\nd. Safety checks:\n   \u25b6 Handle user interrupts within budget\n   \u25b6 Validate initial parent satisfies constraints\n   \u25b6 Time tracking per resolution level\ne. Mathematical guarantees:\n   \u25b6 Integral preservation theorem\n   \u25b6 Bound satisfaction explanation",
